.TH SCANF 3
.SH NAME
scanf, fscanf, sscanf \- formatted input conversion
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <stdio.h>

int
scanf(format [, pointer ] ...)
char *format;

int
fscanf(stream, format [, pointer ] ...)
FILE *stream;
char *format;

int
sscanf(s, format [, pointer ] ...)
char *s, *format;
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B scanf
reads from the standard input,
.B fscanf
from the named
.IR stream ,
and
.B sscanf
from the string
.IR s .
Each reads characters, interprets them as the string
.I format
directs, and stores the results through the
.IR pointer s
that follow it, which must point to variables of the right types.
.PP
The format may hold:
.TP 4
\(bu
White space, which matches any amount of white space in the input,
including none.
.TP
\(bu
Ordinary characters, other than %, each of which must match the next
character of the input.
.TP
\(bu
Conversion specifications, each made of a %; a * if the field is to be
read but not stored; a number, the greatest width of the field; a size,
.B l
or
.BR h ;
and a conversion character.
.PP
An input field is the characters up to the next white space, or as
many as the width allows, whichever comes first; the conversions other
than c and [ skip white space before it. The conversion characters are:
.TP 8
.B d
A decimal integer, with an optional sign; the pointer is to an int.
.TP
.B u
A decimal integer; to an unsigned int.
.TP
.B o
An octal integer; to an int.
.TP
.BR x ", " X
A hexadecimal integer, with or without a leading 0x; to an int.
.TP
.B i
An integer written as in C: hexadecimal after 0x, octal after 0,
otherwise decimal; to an int.
.TP
.BR e ", " f ", " g
A floating point number: an optional sign, digits with an optional
decimal point, and an optional exponent of e or E and an integer; to a
float.
.TP
.B s
A string of characters other than white space; the pointer is to an
array of characters large enough for it and the NUL that is added.
.TP
.B c
Characters: as many as the width, or one if there is no width, white
space included; the pointer is to an array of characters, and no NUL is
added. To read the next character that is not white space, use %1s.
.TP
.B [
A string of the characters between the brackets, as in [abc], or, if
the first is ^, as in [^abc], of the characters not between them; a ]
right after [ or [^ is one of them. A NUL is added.
.TP
.B n
Nothing is read; the number of characters read so far is stored, in an
int.
.TP
.B %
Matches a %.
.PP
.B l
before d, i, o, u or x means that the pointer is to a long, and before
e, f or g, that it is to a double.
.B h
before d, i, o, u or x means that the pointer is to a short.
.SH "RETURN VALUES"
Each returns the number of fields stored, which is less than the
format asks for when the input stops matching it, or EOF if the input
ends before the first field is stored.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
	int i, n;
	long size;
	double x;
	char name[50];

	n = scanf("%d %ld %lf %49s", &i, &size, &x, name);
.fi
.PP
with the input
.PP
.nf
	25 70000 3.5e2 thomas
.fi
.PP
stores 25 in i, 70000 in size, 350.0 in x and "thomas" in name, and
sets n to 4.
.SH NOTES
The floating point conversions are put into a program only when the
program needs them: cc includes them when the program passes a pointer
to a float or a double to a function whose name ends in scanf. Otherwise
there is the message "scanf: floating point conversion not linked in".
.SH SEE ALSO
getc(3), gets(3), atof(3), printf(3), stdio(3)
.SH HISTORY
The
.BR scanf ,
.B fscanf
and
.B sscanf
functions first appeared in Version 7 AT&T UNIX.
.SH BUGS
A string read with s or [ and no width can overrun its array.
.PP
The result says how many fields were stored, not where the input
stopped matching; the characters that did not match are left in the
input, and are read again by the next call.
.PP
Each argument must be a pointer: scanf("%d", n) instead of
scanf("%d", &n) stores at whatever address n holds.
